<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chris Hani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Martin Thembisile Hani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chris Hani was a South African anti-apartheid leader, chief of staff of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umkhonto-we-sizwe">Umkhonto we Sizwe</a> and from 1991 general secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-communist-party">South African Communist Party</a>. Highly popular among young black South Africans, he was assassinated outside his home in Boksburg on 10 April 1993 by a right-wing extremist. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>'s appeal for calm helped keep negotiations on track and an election date was soon fixed.</p></div>
